   $q$Why does the author end by pointing out that many big cities still sit beside rivers?$q$, $q$[{"text":"To argue that people today should stop building their cities near any rivers.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"To start a brand new topic about how highways carry goods between cities.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-structure"},{"text":"To show that rivers were so important that their effect on towns can still be seen.","is_correct":true},{"text":"To prove that cities today still get all of their drinking water from rivers.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$To show that rivers were so important that their effect on towns can still be seen.$q$, $q$The map detail ties the past to today and shows how much rivers shaped where towns grew. It cannot prove cities drink only river water, since the author says we now "pipe water many miles."$q$, 'draft', 'f4ef9bd8-0f01-504e-9f38-09b1906450fb')
